Machine learning uses algorithms to identify patterns in a large swath of data.
For non-parametric tree models, it can make
predictions without relying on a specific functional form or identification. This makes
it particularly powerful when data availability is not in issue. 
For this post specifically, we use gradient boosting, which is a tree-based model
that learn from the error term iteratively. We have a model from the class
as a benchmark, and test whether a refined version of the class model actually
pick better stocks.

## The class experiment
The class experiment sets up teams of two, and each team has two $100,000
portfolios on Investopedia's simulator. Portfolio 1 uses discretion, while 
Portfolio 2 uses indicators like Sharpe ratio, Jensen's alpha, etc. to optimize
the weight or to pick stocks. 

Figure 1 below shows that the mean final value for the discretion-based is $\$101,179$, and that for indicators-based is $\$103,087$, with a mean difference of $+\$1,909$. The band in Figure 1 is the 95% bootstrap interval for the mean difference from $−\$340$ to $+\$4,109$, which includes zero. Therefore, we cannot conclude
that indicators necessarily beat intuition, although 30 out of 45 teams have higher
returns from using indicators and the band runs up to $+\$4,109$.

<iframe src="fig1_class_experiment.html" width="100%" height="570" style="border:none;" loading="lazy" title="Figure 1: indicator portfolio minus intuition portfolio, by team"></iframe>

*Figure 1. [Difference between indicators-based and intuition-based approach]*

## Why stock returns are hard to predict
The price of a stock today reflects investors' expectations of it in the future.
Once the expectations are priced in, what moves the price is unforseen news, such
as an earinings report that beats market expectation.

To put it into context, in an average month, actual returns differ across stocks by about 8.6 points a month, whereas our model’s forecasts differ by only 0.34. 
As such, almost all of a stock's monthly move is news no model could predict
beforehand. More importantly, an observable pattern in past prices attracts
more investors to trade on that pattern. However, the profit in a pattern is the gap
between the current stock price and where the pattern says it's heading. More buying
closes the gap faster and destroys the pattern, so investors have to constantly
seek new patterns.

## The Original Gradient Boosting Model
The original gradient boosting model takes daily prices for 96 large U.S. stocks from 2022, learns from mid-2022 to the end of 2023, then predicts each stock’s return over the next month from 2024 on. On the first trading day of each month it buys the ten stocks with the highest predictions.

Based on the model, $\$1$ turns to $\$2.51$ against the benchmark $\$1.64$. 
It claims a Sharpe ratio of 8.84 and a stagger 330% alpha a year. However,
the right scaling factor should be $\sqrt{12}$ instead of $\sqrt{252}$ because
the returns are monthly. With the right factor, the Sharpe ratio is 1.93, and
the alpha is 15.7%.

It also has three other problems:
1. Uses raw share price and volume as predictors
Raw share price depends on how many shares the company has issued and split into,
so a $\$500$ stock isn’t more expensive than a $\$50$ one in any useful sense. 
Similarly, bigger companies tend to trade more shares. 

2. Static training data
The model was trained on 18 months of data without retraining. However,
there are regime changes from 2022-2023 to 2026, and the patterns hthe model
learns can change.

3. Risk of data leakage / rear-view bias
The 96 stocks are chose in 2026, and hence the portfolio is made of companies
that are large today, which is some information an investor in 2022 couldn't
know. Any strategy that picks from this list starts with some winners built in.

## Three changes to the model

1. New features and a new target
The raw prices are replaced with twelve measures of how a stock has behaved,
including momentum, unusual trading volume, sensitivity to the market, etc. 
Each measure is ranked against the other 95 stocks on the same day so that
the signals mean the same thing across different years. The target is the stock’s return minus 
the average stock’s so the portfolio only needs to know
which stocks will do better than the others.

2 & 3. Tuning and monthly retraining
The window becomes 2011-2023 instead of mid-2022 to 2023. The model is refitted
every month only on things known by then. This avoids data leakage and allows
the model to adjust as patterns fade. The model is tuned to have smaller trees
with slower learning rate. This makes the model less flexible and memorizes
less noise.

## Out-of-sample test of the improved model
The test date is from January 2024 to November 2026. Each month every model ranks the 96 stocks,
and the top ten
are bought in equal amounts the next day and held a month. 
Each month, the correlation between a model's ranking of the stocks and the
ranking of the returns that followed is the rank IC. Zero means that ranking 
carries no information, and one is a perfect ranking. The rank IC shows that
no model ranks stocks reliably, and the improved one (0.024) ranks no better than the class model (0.045.

Figure 2 shows the growth of $\$1$ since Jan 2024. It becomes $\$2.50$ for the improved model, 
$\$2.09$ for the class model , $\$1.69$ for all 96 stocks, and $\$1.66$ for SPY.
Beta for the improved model is 1.63, meaning that 
it moves 1.6% when SPY moves 1%. The volatility is 31% a year against 15% for SPY.
Both higher beta and volatility seem to point at the model including more risk
exposure in exchange for higher return. However, borrowing to hold SPY at the same 1.6× would have made +105%, compared to the model’s +150%. Thus, extra market risk only explains part of the gain. 

When we compare the model with the momentum rule, which made +142%, the correlation
is 0.82. The model held the same volatile chip stocks (Micron and AMD), and its most important inputs were volatility measures. Thus, momentum bet also explains part
of the improved model.

